#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184095 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fcd3c46b-a87f-5ca8-9cf5-d9444a9c8218" :question/math-academy-id "e-8298" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $1\\times4$ matrix $A$ has at most $1$ singular value.\n\n- Any $4\\times2$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- The matrix ![](images/a3/a33b8a531dc91b49f01826cd7fcedaae2ee3144b5205bbe724efa9f29a302b37.png) has one negative singular value." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $1 \\times 4$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $4 \\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times {\\large 1}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 1} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A} =  \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 4}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $A^T$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, $A^T \\! A$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times {\\large 4}}{A^T} \\times \\underset{{\\large 4} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A} = \n\\underset{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2} \\times \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{\\large 2}}{A^T \\! A}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ cannot have a negative singular value.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2b9ef509-4165-5772-81c1-9a3d78424dd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8299" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/9a/9a78a1681842653fabade3354cc5e885f0bfc32eefa3b56e9d1faf716dc5d97b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3fc137a7-9f7a-58de-ac92-1c7c2bdd53f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8300" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix ![](images/5d/5d2eb1db497eeb2f3e24feaa1ac70547a48cc55d1eeea22da91231b1d1275df8.png)" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )^{2}-(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f72538-1df2-5400-9f78-cb8409f95ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367542} {:answer/id #uuid "aaed5f82-08d1-5931-9dc0-ef6782cb00fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367543} {:answer/id #uuid "85459f08-ebd2-5de2-af01-144d2aeb100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367544} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be0251-97f5-5981-bda6-e62b677f0ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367545} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dc7fb6-4c25-5dc1-b337-7a9abd5eaf65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85459f08-ebd2-5de2-af01-144d2aeb100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367544} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b11b93b-5ed4-5c27-8e1f-ae8a252505fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7d5c241-f1dd-539c-bc82-e319298f04d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139500" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}13-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 12 & 13-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (13-\\lambda )(13-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ 169-26\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-26\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "547cd0d6-fd5f-5b94-99cd-d4762af1fc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367549} {:answer/id #uuid "427695ad-2a8a-55af-9742-265b5d33144e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25,5,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367550} {:answer/id #uuid "49754909-9c5a-50e2-bda8-1f655927811b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,5,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367551} {:answer/id #uuid "b92ac663-8b9a-5f7f-855b-b8691c3e6c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367552} {:answer/id #uuid "edca49b3-568f-5e5b-91e9-bd57fa31411b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b92ac663-8b9a-5f7f-855b-b8691c3e6c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},0,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367552} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d25d269-9cbc-53c8-af16-a9f6f0118125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40fb180a-20af-5d07-ba44-09c0a1ca7e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-139512" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda [(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)] & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{0}=0 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c168ee8-34a2-534a-81a5-674995720f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368901} {:answer/id #uuid "1c349c6e-4603-5782-8f21-7c4719e5fb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368902} {:answer/id #uuid "95cfaebc-b7ec-57bd-8f28-c6848b62aaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368903} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd0a115-8f38-5899-b239-7369f96dddbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368904} {:answer/id #uuid "b77485b6-b9b8-589b-8a6d-2e102be4c0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95cfaebc-b7ec-57bd-8f28-c6848b62aaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368903} :answer-field/id #uuid "6075a19f-6e44-5b59-ab31-144bbc2e20f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e856448-81c6-5a27-b63c-c2864d6eca93" :question/math-academy-id "q-95291" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any matrix of the form  $A=[\\begin{aligned}-a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed,  since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0a7ad6-5558-5f51-9126-f39f19237595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183890} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9e5431-c947-52d5-b88f-f11a7f5c6acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183891} {:answer/id #uuid "363f960a-4b25-50ba-8bdd-7daca577f41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183892} {:answer/id #uuid "3f900922-c351-5f17-9f5b-b61ad8326f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183893} {:answer/id #uuid "40ff94f7-18ef-592d-adbb-23867fe8a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183894}] :answer-field/id #uuid "77ea9a2d-3cff-5f1f-a29e-3f00f3b72b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183889}] :question/id #uuid "ca5e4c86-b607-523c-a857-24250b844373" :question/math-academy-id "q-95299" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Any $6 \\cdot 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n2. Any $10 \\cdot 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n3. The matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6d2ba6-6748-5d86-ad31-6de4c2059d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404775} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e34be-dbb9-509d-8f38-b1edb5d428f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404776} {:answer/id #uuid "c625a43d-92ce-5501-800f-971d29033e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404777} {:answer/id #uuid "35eff4e0-c473-5c92-adae-2b1f1c7cb2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404778} {:answer/id #uuid "e916b831-ab56-5276-b823-ee3fad150fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca6d2ba6-6748-5d86-ad31-6de4c2059d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eed5c36-5d57-5b08-a6c1-2725fe122f8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bd4cb7d-93c2-588f-99ae-34346d7bdefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-95323" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000c3642-3e1b-5984-8ef8-49fcb05660c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404782} {:answer/id #uuid "61fec6e7-4529-5f11-a7be-4864e9546fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404783} {:answer/id #uuid "23953fe2-60bd-5e84-a2a9-cbb794f98dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404784} {:answer/id #uuid "f061db93-35ed-57e1-8914-6c1ff91edd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404785} {:answer/id #uuid "c00f8223-5b90-5dca-aa4b-074bcdeb25ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f061db93-35ed-57e1-8914-6c1ff91edd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404785} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb23bf8c-2c0b-5b65-b787-6a4464fa9ee5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06775344-2a92-5205-9f32-2054df8ee5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95337" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2-\\lambda )^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,2,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "684d0d7c-6c21-5d44-b7c3-71eab50414a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404789} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb76c1a-945d-5de9-8a45-072766c1ef8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404790} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a6892e-2b71-58c4-8195-ae2ebdcda46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404791} {:answer/id #uuid "caa0c8c7-3e7b-52f8-87b0-6d3a1f9c7cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404792} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d3a286-9f2e-5033-b1c0-5b409e80ea4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb76c1a-945d-5de9-8a45-072766c1ef8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404790} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a494b6c-01e8-55d5-b122-0aa7d1180b90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae342c4d-42b7-5fdb-833c-f679170c12f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95354" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1-\\lambda  & 0\\end{aligned}|+(1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -(1-\\lambda )+(1-\\lambda )[(2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-1)] & =0 \\\\ -(1-\\lambda )+(1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1-1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fab31e-219e-5d18-813a-a8d06acc3248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415548} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5778c2-893f-5035-ae58-7208c5dc0a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,1,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415556} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8616be-5d4c-5647-8715-109f64e7adf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415558} {:answer/id #uuid "c1276a11-417f-5453-89d9-104681d36b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415563} {:answer/id #uuid "cf324114-3726-57f4-b073-13b1533f0488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf324114-3726-57f4-b073-13b1533f0488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2503f0e3-6a58-532b-b8da-6c772b852974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2512d16d-c375-5bf3-9c3b-0ab4d79702bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95374" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f16480c-d86a-5db9-b802-4ced71c8377c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415549} {:answer/id #uuid "5febb191-7c5d-5f4e-82d6-efc23b4d4485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16,\\,9,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415551} {:answer/id #uuid "73c9cf54-1f9b-52f1-b8ed-dd9e193c12b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415555} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3fcfba-14a5-5b55-ba3e-4a632953d6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415560} {:answer/id #uuid "be6dbb3a-d9f4-5182-87d2-f89ac41af4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d3fcfba-14a5-5b55-ba3e-4a632953d6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b838eb18-ab2d-5fe6-b6c3-92ea8e153a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ba2e577-1f2d-586f-a5db-7d03c7c71c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-95375" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )[(5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)]-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +16)-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
